      en: "Wadi Hanifa is a 120-kilometer-long valley that runs through the center of Riyadh and was historically the lifeline for the region's settlements. In recent decades, it has undergone one of the world's largest ecological restoration projects, transforming a neglected industrial wasteland into a thriving natural park. This project won the prestigious Aga Khan Award for Architecture for its innovative use of natural processes to treat urban wastewater. Today, the wadi offers extensive parks, walking trails, and picnic areas, providing a green oasis in the heart of the desert metropolis. It serves as a natural drainage system for the city and supports a rich variety of flora and fauna that have returned after the restoration. Significant historical sites, such as the ruins of Diriyah, the ancestral home of the Saudi state, are located along its banks. Several dams, including the Al-Hayer Dam, regulate the water flow and create permanent lakes that support local wildlife. Wadi Hanifa is a unique example of sustainable urban planning, successfully blending historical heritage with modern ecological engineering.",
